### STEP 7 -- 🔴 an incident caused by this tester's own tooling, and what it cost

This is recorded in full because it put four failed logins on a bench account and because the
fix is now in `tools/`.

**What happened.** Between STEP 6(a) and 6(b) the restored-server leg was first run as
`... --password-stdin` with the password piped in, in a shell where `CK_PASSWORD_U3` was also
exported. `tools/tb` had been extended that morning to forward `CK_*` secrets to the box over
**stdin** so they never touch argv. But `subprocess.run(input=...)` **replaces** stdin rather
than prepending to it, so the piped password was discarded and `devclient` read **an empty
string**. It sent an empty password; the far end re-prompted; and `devclient`'s recovery loop
then typed `exit` at the password prompt -- which is itself a password guess.

**What it cost**, from swi_b's own log:
```
2026 Oct 21 00:30:39 authpriv.notice awplus sshd-session[21402]: pam_unix(vty0:auth): authentication failure; ... rhost=10.38.22.97  user=cktmp
2026 Oct 21 00:30:42 authpriv.notice awplus sshd-session[21439]: pam_unix(vty0:auth): authentication failure; ... rhost=10.38.22.97  user=cktmp
2026 Oct 21 00:30:55 authpriv.notice awplus sshd-session[21490]: pam_unix(vty0:auth): authentication failure; ... rhost=10.38.22.97  user=cktmp
2026 Oct 21 00:31:11 authpriv.notice awplus sshd-session[21400]: pam_unix(vty0:auth): authentication failure; ... rhost=10.38.22.97  user=cktmp
2026 Oct 21 00:31:13 auth.notice  awplus sshd-session[21400]: Failed password for cktmp from 10.38.22.97 port 60774 ssh2
```
**Four failed logins, all on the THROWAWAY account `cktmp` on swi_b.** AW+ locks at five, so it
did not lock -- the next attempt, at `00:33:28`, reads `Accepted keyboard-interactive/pam for
cktmp`. **Zero failed logins on `manager`, on any unit, at any point in this group**, which is
exactly what the throwaway account exists to guarantee. The incident was reported to the
sentinel immediately with the count, as instructed.

The console was **not** left unusable: `tools/tb peek tb277 ckA1159 u2 115200` immediately
afterwards read `/dev/u2 TAIL: 'awplus#'`. `devclient` had reported it stuck because the ssh
`~.` escape landed just *after* its last check.

**Three fixes, committed with this case:**
1. `tools/tb` now appends the caller's own stdin **after** the secret lines instead of
   discarding it.
2. `devclient` gained **`--password-env NAME`**, which is the safe path through the wrapper: the
   password rides the same stdin channel as the other secrets and arrives as an environment
   variable, so there is no stdin collision to get wrong. `tools/tb`'s secret pattern was
   widened from `CK_(USER|PASSWORD)(_U<n>)?` to any `CK_PASSWORD*` / `CK_USER*` name.
3. `devclient` now **refuses to send an empty password** when one was requested via
   `--password-env` or `--password-stdin`, its recovery **never types a word at a login or
   password prompt** (at a credential prompt the only keystrokes it sends are the protocol
   escape), and it re-checks once after the escape before declaring the console stuck.

STEP 6(b) above is the re-run with `--password-env`, which worked first time.

**STEP 7 verdict: not a product result.** Recorded as a tester defect, found, fixed and
re-measured.

